<title>NORWAY: Saga 1996 net profits jump 42 pct.</title>
<headline>Saga 1996 net profits jump 42 pct.</headline>
<dateline>OSLO 1997-02-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Twelve months to December 31.</p>
<p>				 (millions of crowns unless stated)</p>
<p> Operating profit		 3,316	vs	  1,864</p>
<p>Profits before tax</p>
<p>   and extraordinary items    3,468	vs	  2,015</p>
<p> After-tax profits		  786	vs	    555</p>
<p>NOTE - Full name of Norway's largest listed oil company is Saga Petroleum ASA. A Reuter poll of nine analysts forecast an operating result of 3.31 billion crowns and pre-tax profits of 3.38 billion crowns. Two analysts recommended to buy the share, six said hold and one said sell.  </p>
<p>Saga's 1996 results do not include UK-based offshore group Santa Fe, which it bought at the end of the year.</p>
<p> Operating revenues	     7,416	vs	  5,621</p>
<p> Operating costs		  4,100	vs	  3,757</p>
<p> Taxes				1,144	vs	  1,460</p>
<p> Extraordinary items	    1,538	vs	    --XX</p>
<p>XX -- Saga said that as a result of a change to Norwegian accounting principles all exploration costs are now expensed. The effect of previously capitalised exploration costs is recorded as extraordinary costs (NOK 1.54 billion).</p>
<p>--Oslo newsroom, +47 22 42 50 41</p>
</text>
